Wlabel = "TelnetU Help"
Contents = TelnetU,TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Commands,TelnetU Window
Name = TelnetU
Llabel = " TELNETU"
Slabel = "TELNETU"
Branch = TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Commands,TelnetU Window
Title = "TelnetU - VT100/UTF-8 Terminal"
Text =
TelnetU logs you in to another computer on the network, as \
telnet does, but it has a terminal of its own: a VT100 and \
xterm, with UTF-8. Box lines, blocks, accents, Greek and \
Cyrillic letters, and the art of BBSes (CP437) come out right. \
So today's telnet hosts look as they should: BBSes, MUDs, \
nethack.alt.org, telehack.com, mapscii.me.

A box asks for the host. Type its name or its address, then \
press Return or Enter. A port may follow: 10.0.2.2 2323. \
Press Cmd for the list of known hosts: choose one with the \
arrows and Return, or click it. Names that are not in \
/etc/hosts are looked up with DNS. With no name, you get the \
telnet> prompt. Exit closes the box.

The TelnetU menu has 6 ways to run it. Full Screen fills the \
screen. Window is a window with a border, which you can move \
and resize. Each comes with the system font, the small font \
or the narrow font (more columns). When you leave TelnetU, \
you come back here.

In a UNIX System window, type: telnetu host [port]. \
The old telnet is still there: telnet host. \EOT\

Name = TelnetU Keys
Llabel = " TELNETU  KEYS"
Slabel = "  KEYS"
Branch = TelnetU,TelnetU Commands,TelnetU Window
Title = "TelnetU Keys"
Text =
Exit gives the telnet> prompt at the bottom of the window. \
There, Return takes you back to the host, and Exit again \
closes the connection and the window.

Cancl, or the X box at the bottom left of the window, closes \
the connection and the window at once.

^] does the same as Exit. So does ~ at the start of a line: \
~quit closes the connection, and ~~ sends one ~.

Help lists the telnet> commands. Rfrsh draws the screen again. \
Break sends a telnet BREAK to the host.

The arrow keys, Page, Shift-Page, Home, End and F1 to F8 go to \
the host. Shift-F1 to Shift-F8 are F9 to F16. \
A mouse click goes to hosts that ask for the mouse. \EOT\

Name = TelnetU Commands
Llabel = " TELNETUCOMMANDS"
Slabel = "COMMANDS"
Branch = TelnetU,TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Window
Title = "TelnetU Commands"
Text =
At the telnet> prompt, type a command and press Return:

open host [port]   connect to a host
close              close the connection
quit               close it and leave TelnetU
z                  a UNIX shell (type exit to come back)
send x             ayt brk ip ao ec el eof nop escape
charset c          auto utf8 latin1 cp437
term types         the terminal types to give the host
status             what is going on
?                  all the commands \EOT\

Name = TelnetU Window
Llabel = " TELNETU WINDOW"
Slabel = " WINDOW"
Branch = TelnetU,TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Commands
Title = "TelnetU Window, Fonts and Sizes"
Text =
A Window has a border. To move it, use the box at its top \
left; to resize it, the box at its bottom right. The host \
gets the new size. The ? box is the Help key, and the X box \
closes TelnetU.

In a UNIX System window:
telnetu [-w] [-f font] [-g CxL] host [port]
-w        a window with a border
-f font   system 9x12, small 6x10, narrow 5x10
-g CxL    columns x lines, such as -g 100x30

At the telnet> prompt, these change it at once:
window, full (no border), font name, size CxL.

Without a border, at most: system 80x24, small 120x28, \
narrow 144x28. With one: 76x20, 114x24, 137x24. \EOT\
